/// The Length built-in counts a collection's elements.
///
/// The interpreter's built-in renders its argument first and measures the
/// rendering: Str([1, 2]) is '[1, 2]', six characters.  Compiled code returns
/// the element count, which is the answer no reading disputes.
///
/// ⚠️ This is the rare reproduction where the NORMATIVE half is the wrong one.
/// Everywhere else the interpreter defines the language and a compiled
/// disagreement is the compiler's fault; here it is the interpreter's built-in
/// that has to change.  So this file passes compiled and fails interpreted --
/// the mirror of EnumMemberSymbol.
///
/// The property form is asserted alongside because it is correct under both,
/// and it is what a program should use until this is fixed.
///
/// Length (S) on a String is a different path and is correct under both; it is
/// asserted here so a fix aimed at the collection case cannot quietly break it.
///
/// Fails with: Assertion 'left = right' failed.  Expected '2' but got '6'.
///
test 'The Length built-in counts elements, not characters';
begin
    var Pair := [1, 2];

    AssertEqual (2, Pair.Length);
    AssertEqual (2, Length (Pair));
    AssertEqual (5, Length ('hello'));
end
